Z||000000||FieroHarry||06-24-2001||12:06 PM||||Hey everybody,<BR>Ive replaced that accursed seal once before and it still leaks, but i did it with a used seal (emergant repairs).<BR>I now have the new seal and before I put this one in I have to ask,<P>How many of you have heard of the drilled holes (by the seal) and if any of you have done this?<P>Is it worth doing?<BR>Will it screw my pillars up in the long run from corrosion (im sure it has too)?<P>Thanks for any responses<BR><P>------------------<BR><B>Harry Waggoner</B><BR>1985 GT 266K+ <I>FOCOSEVA,FOCOA</I><BR><B>"If you want to blend in, take the bus!"</B>||||reg||9
Z||000001||cadaver||06-24-2001||01:51 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroHarry:<BR><B><P>Is it worth doing?<BR>Will it screw my pillars up in the long run from corrosion (im sure it has too)?<P>Thanks for any responses<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>I think it is worth doing unless you like a wet seat.<BR>Pontiac figured it was an easy way to fix the problem.<BR>The space frame under the roof is a wet panel anyway. You see the split in the roof panel between the front and back. Water get in there. I dont think it would corrode any more than any other exposed part of it.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Rodrv6||06-24-2001||02:20 PM||||I had my roof panel off a couple of years ago and found no sign of any corrosion under there.  All the steel substructure seems to be well primed from the factory.  My sunroof had been leaking for quite a while, so I know that a lot of water had gotten down there.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles :)||||reg||1
